Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Code of Conduct for Judges

Fa Fa [2010] No. 54

To the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Code of Conduct for Judges is hereby issued to you for earnest implementation and enforcement. The Code of Conduct for Judges (Trial) issued by the Supreme People's Court on November 4, 2005, shall be simultaneously abolished.

December 6, 2010

Code of Conduct for Judges

(Issued for Trial by the Supreme People's Court on November 4, 2005; revised and officially issued for implementation on December 6, 2010)

To vigorously promote the core judicial values of "impartiality, integrity, and serving the people," standardize the basic conduct of judges, and establish a good professional image of the judiciary, this Code is formulated in accordance with the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China, the Civil Service Law of the People's Republic of China, and other laws.

I. General Provisions

Article 1. Loyalty and Firmness. Adhere to the supremacy of the Party's cause, the interests of the people, and the Constitution and laws; maintain consistency with the Party Central Committee in both thought and action; and refrain from any words or deeds that violate the basic policies of the Party and the State or the socialist judicial system.

Article 2. Impartial Adjudication. Adhere to the facts as the basis and the law as the criterion; treat all parties equally; ensure substantive justice, procedural justice, and image justice; strive to achieve the organic unity of the legal effect and social effect of case handling; and refrain from abusing power or perverting the law in rendering judgments.

Article 3. Efficient Case Handling. Cultivate a sense of efficiency; arrange work scientifically and reasonably; perform duties promptly within the statutory time limits; strive to improve the efficiency of case handling; and refrain from causing delays without cause, hindering work, or wasting judicial resources.

Article 4. Clean and Honest. Comply with all rules on integrity; refrain from using the position and status of a judge to seek improper benefits; refrain from introducing agents ad litem, defense counsel, or intermediary agencies to the parties; refrain from referring case sources to lawyers or other persons or providing them with other improper assistance.

Article 5. Wholeheartedly Serving the People. Implement all provisions and requirements for serving the people through justice; listen to the voices of the people, observe their circumstances, and understand their intentions; adhere to active justice; establish a service-oriented mindset; provide convenient services such as litigation guidance, risk warnings, and legal clarifications; and avoid undesirable work styles such as being "cold, harsh, overbearing, or perfunctory."

Article 6. Strictly Observing Discipline. Comply with all disciplinary rules; refrain from disclosing State secrets, commercial secrets, or personal privacy obtained during trial work; refrain from inquiring into, interfering with, or influencing cases being tried by others; and refrain from casually expressing opinions that undermine the seriousness and authoritativeness of effective judgments.

Article 7. Dedication and Commitment. Love the people's judicial cause; enhance professional mission and sense of honor; strengthen professional study; improve judicial competence; faithfully perform duties; work tirelessly without complaint; and contribute selflessly; and refrain from slackness, negligence, or dereliction of duty.

Article 8. Strengthening Self-Cultivation. Persist in learning and continuously improve one's own quality; observe judicial etiquette; comply with dress code regulations; use civil language; behave appropriately; refrain from heavy makeup or wearing accessories incompatible with the status of a judge; and refrain from participating in activities that damage the professional image of the judiciary.

II. Filing of Cases

Article 9. Basic Requirements

(1) Guarantee the parties' right to file a lawsuit in accordance with the law, with special attention to the litigation needs of women, children, the elderly, and persons with disabilities;
(2) Facilitate the people's access to litigation and reduce the burden on the parties;
(3) Ensure the quality and efficiency of case filing.

Article 10. Party Comes to Court to File a Lawsuit

(1) Strengthen litigation guidance and provide litigation guidance materials;
(2) If the conditions for filing a lawsuit are met, file the case promptly within the statutory time limit;
(3) If the conditions are not met, reject the case and inform the reasons; if the party insists on filing, render a ruling rejecting the case;
(4) After the case has been filed, do not force the party to withdraw the lawsuit;
(5) If the party voluntarily waives the lawsuit, permission shall generally be granted, unless otherwise provided by law.

Article 11. Party Files a Lawsuit Orally

(1) Inform the party that a written complaint should be submitted;
(2) If the party cannot write a complaint and has difficulty entrusting another person to write it on their behalf, request that they clarify the claims, truthfully provide the case facts and contact information, record this in the笔录, read it back to them, and after confirmation of accuracy, have them sign or fingerprint the record.

Article 12. Party Requests On-Site Case Filing or Remote Case Filing

(1) If the party is unable to come to court to file a lawsuit due to physical disability causing mobility difficulties, serious illness confining them to bed, or other reasons, and lacks the capacity to appoint an agent, the court may, based on actual circumstances, accept the case materials on-site;
(2) If the party's residence is far from the accepting court and the case facts are clear, the legal relationship is unambiguous, and disputes are minor, the court may accept the case materials via the internet or by mail;
(3) For parties who do not meet the above conditions, they shall be informed to file the lawsuit at the court.

Article 13. Party Files a Lawsuit at a People's Tribunal

If the People's Tribunal has jurisdiction to accept the case, it shall accept the case materials and shall not require the party to file the case at the case filing division of the basic-level people's court to which it belongs.

Article 14. Case Is Not Within the Jurisdiction of the Court or the Accepting Court

(1) Inform the party of the reasons why the case is not within the jurisdiction of the court or why the accepting court lacks jurisdiction;
(2) Based on the actual circumstances of the case, indicate the competent authority or the court that has jurisdiction;
(3) If the party insists on filing, render a ruling rejecting the case, and shall not accept the case in violation of jurisdictional rules.

Article 15. A Case That Should Be Prosecuted as a Public Prosecution Is Brought as a Private Prosecution

(1) After accepting the case, it shall be transferred to the competent authority for handling, and the party shall be informed;
(2) If the circumstances are urgent, emergency measures shall be taken first, and then the case shall be transferred to the competent authority and the party informed.

Article 16. The Content or Form of the Complaint Does Not Comply with Regulations

(1) Inform the party to make corrections in accordance with relevant regulations, explaining the requirements at one time;
(2) Do not refuse to file a case due to defects other than the statutory requirements for filing.

Article 17. Insufficient Evidence in the Case Materials

In principle, do not refuse to file a case on the grounds that the evidence supporting the claims is insufficient.

Article 18. Encountering Complex or Difficult Situations Where a Decision on Whether to File Cannot Be Made on the Spot

(1) Accept the materials and issue a receipt, informing the party to await the review result;
(2) Promptly review the case and notify the party of the result within the statutory time limit.

Article 19. Discovering Disputes Involving Groups or That Are Prone to Escalation

Promptly report to the leadership and contact relevant departments, actively engage in mediation efforts, and prevent the dispute from escalating.

Article 20. Party Inquires About Substantive Issues Such as Whether Evidence Is Valid or Whether They Will Win the Case After Filing

(1) Do not provide any opinion that could be seen as favoring one side;
(2) Inform the party that such issues can only be determined through trial and that they should trust the court to render a fair judgment.

Article 21. Party Inquires About the Case Handling Process or Time After Filing

Inform the party of the case handling process and statutory time limits, and do not refuse to answer on the grounds that it is unrelated to the case filing work.

Article 22. Party Pays Litigation Fees in Advance

(1) Strictly determine the amount in accordance with regulations; do not charge extra fees or arbitrarily reduce fees;
(2) If payment must be made at a designated bank, promptly inform the party of the account number and location;
(3) If it is truly necessary for the People's Tribunal to collect the fee itself, a receipt shall be issued in accordance with regulations.

Article 23. Party Fails to Pay Litigation Fees Promptly

(1) If the party meets the conditions for judicial assistance, inform them that they may apply for a deferral or reduction of litigation fees;
(2) If the party does not meet the conditions for judicial assistance, notify them in writing to pay the fee within a specified period, and inform them that if they fail to pay the fee without justifiable reason within the time limit, the case will be treated as withdrawn.

Article 24. Party Applies for Pre-Litigation Property Preservation, Evidence Preservation, or Other Measures

(1) Strictly examine the conditions and reasons for the application, and promptly render a ruling in accordance with the law;
(2) If a ruling to take preservation or other measures is made, enforce it promptly in accordance with the law; if the application does not meet the conditions, patiently explain the reasons;
(3) Do not abuse pre-litigation property preservation, evidence preservation, or other measures.

Article 25. Party Entrusts a Judicial Appraisal Independently or Applies for the Court to Entrust One

(1) If the parties have reached an agreement to entrust an appraisal independently, the appraisal shall be carefully reviewed; appraisal opinions that are procedurally lawful and substantively fair shall be accepted; if the appraisal opinion does not meet the requirements, a new appraisal may be requested, explaining the reasons;
(2) If a party applies for the court to entrust an appraisal, the court shall promptly decide whether to grant the application and respond to the party; if judicial appraisal is permitted, an appraisal institution shall be entrusted in accordance with regulations to conduct the appraisal promptly.

III. Court Trial

Article 26. Basic Requirements

(1) Standardize trial conduct and establish a good image;
(2) Enhance the ability to control the trial and ensure the quality of adjudication;
(3) Strictly follow trial procedures and equally protect the litigation rights of the parties;
(4) Maintain trial order and ensure the smooth progress of trial activities.

Article 27. Preparations Before the Trial Session

(1) Notify all parties of the trial time and place promptly within the statutory time limit;
(2) For public hearings, a notice shall be published promptly within the statutory time limit;
(3) If a party applies for a closed hearing, the court shall promptly review the application; if it meets the statutory conditions, permission shall be granted; if it does not, the trial shall be held in public and the reasons shall be explained;
(4) If pre-trial evidence exchange is necessary, the court shall promptly remind the parties and proactively inform them of the time limit for presenting evidence;
(5) If a party applies for the court to collect evidence, and it is truly evidence that the party cannot collect on its own, the court shall promptly investigate and collect it without delay; if the evidence cannot be obtained, the court shall proactively inform the party of the reasons; if the evidence is collectible by the party on its own, the court shall inform the party to collect it;
(6) Consciously comply with the legal provisions and relevant rules on withdrawal; if the court does not agree to a party's request for withdrawal, it shall explain the reasons to the party;
(7) For cases where the parties are emotionally charged and disputes are prone to escalation, prepare a work plan before the trial to prevent violent incidents.

Article 28. Need to Change the Scheduled Trial Time

(1) Do not change the trial time without cause;
(2) If an extension is truly necessary due to special circumstances, immediately notify the parties and other participants in the proceedings;
(3) If notification is impossible, arrange for personnel to be present at the originally scheduled trial time and place to explain the reasons to the parties and other participants.

Article 29. Points to Note When Appearing in Court

(1) Appear in court punctually, do not arrive late, leave early, or be absent;
(2) Before entering the courtroom, change into a judge's uniform or robe, which must be kept neat and dignified; wear of civilian clothes in court is strictly prohibited; members of the collegial panel shall wear uniform attire;
(3) If a judge's passage is available, use it;
(4) Generally, enter the courtroom after the parties, agents, defense counsel, prosecutors, etc., have entered, except when the aforementioned persons are late or refuse to appear;
(5) Do not greet the litigation parties casually, and do not engage in particularly intimate words or actions with any one side;
(6) Appearing in court after consuming alcohol is strictly prohibited.

Article 30. Words and Actions During the Trial

(1) Sit upright and avoid all unseemly postures;
(2) Concentrate, focus on the trial, and do not engage in activities unrelated to the trial;
(3) Do not smoke, chat, or doze off on the bench; do not answer or make phone calls; do not leave the bench without cause;
(4) Treat all persons related to the trial activities equally, and do not show favoritism toward any party in the litigation;
(5) Politely signal the parties and other participants to speak;
(6) Do not use biased language in questioning, and do not argue with the parties or other participants;
(7) Use the gavel strictly in accordance with regulations; the force of striking the gavel should be sufficient for the public gallery to hear.

Article 31. Allocation and Control of Speaking Time for Litigation Parties

(1) Fairly and reasonably allocate the speaking time for statements and arguments among the litigation parties based on the case circumstances and trial needs;
(2) Do not arbitrarily interrupt the statements of the parties, agents, or defense counsel;
(3) If the opinions expressed by the parties, agents, or defense counsel are repetitive or irrelevant to the case, appropriately remind them to stop, and do not criticize them with harsh language.

Article 32. Party Uses a Dialect or Minority Language

(1) If a party can only speak a dialect, this shall be permitted; if the other party states that they do not understand the dialect, a person proficient in the dialect may be used to repeat the statement in standard Chinese, and the repetition shall be accurate;
(2) If a statement is made in a minority language and the other party states that they do not understand it, an interpreter shall be provided.

Article 33. Party Is Emotionally Agitated, Shouting for Justice or Complaining of Injustice in Court

(1) Reiterate that the party must abide by court discipline and that the court will allow them time to speak in accordance with the law;
(2) If the party does not heed the warning, take timely measures to stop them;
(3) If the measures are ineffective, take appropriate action in accordance with relevant regulations.

Article 34. Disputes or Personal Attacks Occur Among the Litigation Parties

(1) Immediately stop the dispute, criticize and educate the parties, and do not favor any one side;
(2) Warn all parties that they must make statements in order and focus on the case;
(3) If they do not heed the warning, take appropriate action in accordance with relevant regulations.

Article 35. Party Signs the Trial Record

(1) Inform the party of the legal effect of the trial record, hand it to them for reading; if they are unable to read, read it to them, and after confirmation of accuracy, have them sign or fingerprint;
(2) If the party points out omissions or errors in the record, after verification, make immediate corrections and have the party sign or fingerprint at the correction site; if there are no omissions or errors and corrections should not be made, record the party's request in the record;
(3) Do not require the party to sign or fingerprint without having them read or verified the record;
(4) If the party waives the right to read or verify, request that they sign or fingerprint; if the party neither reads nor signs or fingerprints, record the circumstances in the record.

Article 36. Points to Note When Pronouncing Judgment

(1) Judgment pronunciation shall always be conducted in open court;
(2) When pronouncing judgment, members of the collegial panel or the sole judge shall stand; the written judgment shall be read in a loud, clear, and accurate voice;
(3) If judgment is pronounced in court on the same day, the ruling matters shall be announced, the reasons briefly explained, and the statutory time limit for service of the written judgment shall be informed;
(4) If judgment is pronounced on a scheduled date, the written judgment shall be served immediately after the pronouncement;
(5) After the pronouncement, do not praise or criticize any litigation party; patiently explain in response to questions raised by the parties.

Article 37. Case Cannot Be Concluded Within the Trial Time Limit

(1) If an extension of the trial time limit is needed, complete the approval procedures in accordance with regulations;
(2) Within a reasonable time before the expiry of the trial time limit or before the conversion of procedures, promptly inform the parties and other participants of the reasons why the case cannot be concluded.

Article 38. People's Procuratorate Lodges a Protest

(1) File the case in accordance with the law and conduct the trial in accordance with relevant regulations;
(2) Provide necessary conditions and conveniences for the procuratorial personnel, defense counsel, and litigation representatives to review case files and photocopy file materials.

IV. Mediation During Litigation

Article 39. Basic Requirements

(1) Establish a mediation mindset, enhance mediation awareness, adhere to "mediation first, combination of mediation and adjudication," and fully leverage the role of mediation in resolving disputes;
(2) Earnestly follow the principles of legality and voluntariness, and prevent improper mediation or one-sided pursuit of mediation rates;
(3) Pay attention to methods and techniques, improve mediation capabilities, and strive to achieve case settlement and resolution of disputes.

Article 40. Contact with Parties During Mediation

(1) Solicit the mediation willingness of each party;
(2) Based on the specific circumstances of the case, mediation work may be conducted separately with each party;
(3) When contacting one party, maintain fairness to avoid reasonable doubts from the other party about the judge's neutrality.

Article 41. Only the Party's Agent Participates in Mediation

(1) Carefully examine whether the agent has special authorization; if so, the agent may directly participate in mediation;
(2) If there is no special authorization, the agent may participate in mediation, but if a mediation agreement is reached, it must be signed or sealed by the party, or the party may later complete the special authorization and ratification procedures; if necessary, the party may be required to participate in mediation in person.

Article 42. One Party Indicates Unwillingness to Mediate

(1) If there is a possibility of mediation, use various methods to actively guide mediation;
(2) If the party insists on not mediating, do not force mediation.

Article 43. Mediation Agreement Harms the Interests of Others

(1) Inform the party participating in mediation that clauses concerning the rights and obligations of others should be corrected;
(2) If it is discovered that the mediation agreement harms the interests of others, the validity of such clauses shall not be confirmed.

Article 44. During Mediation, a Party Requests an Opinion on the Issue of Liability

Opinions shall be expressed based on the facts of the case, legal provisions, and the actual needs of mediation, paying attention to methods and techniques, and striving to facilitate the parties' agreement on a mediation plan.

Article 45. Disagreement Among Parties on the Mediation Plan

(1) Continue coordinating efforts to narrow the differences between the parties as much as possible, allowing them to make new choices and strive for mediation settlement;
(2) If the differences are significant and mediation is truly difficult, render a judgment promptly in accordance with the law.

V. Preparation of Written Instruments

Article 46. Basic Requirements

(1) Strictly adhere to the format and specifications, improve the ability to draft written judgments, ensure the quality of written judgments, and maintain the seriousness and authority of written judgments;
(2) Written judgments in ordinary procedure cases shall be comprehensive in content, thorough in reasoning, logically rigorous, standardized in terminology, and concise in wording;
(3) Written judgments in summary procedure cases shall be concise, accurate, and standardized;
(4) Written judgments in cases tried by a collegial panel shall reflect the opinions of the majority.

Article 47. Responsibility for the Quality of Written Judgments

(1) The judge handling the case or the sole judge bears primary responsibility for the quality of the written judgment; other members of the collegial panel bear secondary responsibility;
(2) The judge responsible for reviewing and signing the written judgment shall conduct a strict and meticulous review.

Article 48. Description of the Trial Procedure and the Entire Trial Process

(1) Accurately describe the names of the parties, the cause of action, the date of case filing, the date of trial, and the situations of the participants in the proceedings;
(2) If the summary procedure is converted to ordinary procedure, the time and reasons for the conversion shall be stated;
(3) If parties are added or substituted, the time, reasons, and other circumstances of the addition or substitution shall be stated;
(4) Important matters such as the process for handling jurisdictional objections, entrusting judicial appraisal, evaluation, audit, and extension of the trial period shall be truthfully described.

Article 49. Summary of the Complaints and Defenses of the Litigation Parties

(1) Briefly and accurately summarize the claims and defenses of each party;
(2) The space allocated to each party shall be fair and reasonable.

Article 50. Description of the Parties' Evidence Examination Process and Disputed Issues

50. Pre-trial Evidence Exchange and Court Trial:
(1) Briefly describe the process of evidence exchange and court trial;
(2) Accurately summarize the disputed issues;
(3) Where the facts or legal relationship are complex, narrate in sections and paragraphs based on the accurately summarized disputed issues.

Article 51. Narration of Fact-Finding in Ordinary Procedures
(1) The expression shall be objective, the logic rigorous, and the wording precise, avoiding obviously favorable or derogatory terms;
(2) Accurately analyze and explain the reasons for accepting or rejecting evidence submitted by the parties, and the facts that the accepted evidence can prove;
(3) Provide reasonable explanations on issues of burden of proof, probative value of evidence, and standard of proof.

Article 52. Analysis and Argumentation on the Nature and Outcome of Ordinary Procedure Cases
(1) Provide accurate, objective, and concise reasoning; state the reasons for adopting or not adopting defense opinions, representation opinions, etc.;
(2) In criminal cases, render a conviction or acquittal based on the relevant provisions of laws and judicial interpretations and the specific facts of the case; if convicted, analyze and determine circumstances of heavier, lighter, mitigated, or exempted punishment that are statutory or discretionary;
(3) In civil cases, based on the relevant provisions of laws, regulations, and judicial interpretations, combined with the specific circumstances of the individual case, clarify the legal relationship of the case, and provide a detailed summary and evaluation of the rights, obligations, liability, and the extent of liability between the parties;
(4) In administrative cases, based on the relevant provisions of laws, regulations, and judicial interpretations, combined with the facts of the case, analyze and argue whether the specific administrative acts of the administrative organs and their staff are lawful, whether the plaintiff's lawful rights and interests have been infringed, and whether there is a causal relationship with the specific administrative act being sued.

Article 53. Citation of Legal Provisions
(1) In the reasoning section, the original text of the legal provisions shall be cited, and the citations must extend to the article, paragraph, and subparagraph;
(2) Where multiple disputes are involved in the reasoning, each argument shall be accompanied by a citation;
(3) When finally citing the legal basis in the reasoning of the main text of the judgment, only the article numbers of the legal provisions shall be cited.

Article 54. Errors Discovered After Announcement or Service of Judgment Documents
(1) For ordinary textual errors or ungrammatical sentences, promptly explain the situation to the parties, retrieve the judgment document, and correct it with a correction seal or re-issue the judgment document;
(2) For significant textual errors or ungrammatical sentences, if the document can be immediately retrieved, do so and re-issue it; if it cannot be immediately retrieved, issue a ruling to correct.

VI. Enforcement

Article 55. Basic Requirements
(1) Enforce promptly and effectively in accordance with the law, ensuring the seriousness and authority of effective legal documents, and protecting the lawful rights and interests of the parties;
(2) Adhere to civilized enforcement, strictly take enforcement measures in accordance with the law, and resolutely avoid inaction and arbitrary action;
(3) Pay attention to methods and approaches, focusing on both the legal and social effects of enforcement.

Article 56. Enforcement Respondent Requesting Negotiation through Agent on the Basis of Special Authorization
(1) Decide whether to contact the enforcement respondent's agent based on what is beneficial for enforcement;
(2) If it is truly necessary to contact the enforcement respondent personally, inform the enforcement respondent of the obligation to cooperate with the court's enforcement work and not to make excuses.

Article 57. Enforcement Applicant Calling or Visiting to Inquire about the Enforcement Status
(1) Keep thorough records and promptly explain the progress of enforcement;
(2) If the enforcement applicant requests to inspect relevant case files, permission shall be granted, except where confidentiality is required by law.

Article 58. Relevant Party Requesting Return of Original Materials
After verifying the copies submitted by the party, the originals shall be returned, and the party shall sign or seal the copies for archiving and future reference.

Article 59. Locating the Property of the Enforcement Respondent
(1) If the enforcement applicant provides the court with clues about the enforcement respondent's property, investigate promptly, take appropriate enforcement measures in accordance with the law, and inform the enforcement applicant of the relevant situation;
(2) Actively investigate the enforcement respondent's property ex officio and promptly take appropriate enforcement measures in accordance with the law.

Article 60. Enforcement Party Requests Settlement
(1) Promptly convey the settlement request to the opposing party, objectively explain the difficulty and risk of enforcement in an appropriate manner, and facilitate a settlement between the enforcement parties;
(2) If the party refuses settlement, continue enforcement in accordance with the law;
(3) If the enforcement applicant and enforcement respondent reach a settlement, prepare a written settlement agreement and file it, or record the terms of the oral settlement agreement in the minutes, and have both parties sign or seal it.

Article 61. Suspension, Termination, and Conclusion of Enforcement
(1) Take measures of suspension, termination, or conclusion of enforcement strictly in accordance with the statutory conditions and procedures;
(2) Inform the enforcement applicant of the facts and relevant legal provisions on which the suspension, termination, or conclusion is based, and patiently provide explanations;
(3) Inform the enforcement applicant of the conditions and procedures for resuming enforcement after suspension or termination;
(4) If the suspension, termination, or conclusion of enforcement is erroneous, correct it promptly in accordance with the law.

Article 62. Enforcement Respondent Objecting to Jurisdiction of the Entrusted Court
(1) Review whether the case meets the conditions for entrusted enforcement; if not, promptly report to the leadership and take appropriate corrective measures;
(2) If the conditions for entrusted enforcement are met, inform the enforcement respondent of the basis for the entrusted court's acceptance of enforcement and proceed with enforcement in accordance with the law.

Article 63. Third Party Objecting to Enforcement
(1) Require the third party to provide evidentiary materials for the objection and promptly review them;
(2) Depending on the specific circumstances, take restrictive measures on the property under enforcement, refraining from disposal temporarily;
(3) If the objection is established, take appropriate corrective measures; if the objection is not established, reject it in accordance with the law.

Article 64. Sealing, Seizing, Freezing, Auctioning, or Selling the Property of the Enforcement Respondent
(1) Strictly follow procedures; do not seal, seize, or freeze property beyond the claim amount or value;
(2) Carefully prepare a detailed list of the property subject to measures, recording the type and quantity, and have the parties sign or seal to confirm;
(3) Strictly comply with the relevant provisions on auction and sale, entrust appraisal and auction institutions in accordance with the law, and do not harm the lawful interests of the parties.

Article 65. Collection of Enforcement Funds
(1) Enforcement funds shall be directly transferred to the dedicated account for enforcement funds;
(2) If the enforcement respondent delivers cash or negotiable instruments on the spot, the enforcement personnel shall, together with the enforcement respondent, deliver the cash or instruments to the court's finance department and promptly issue a receipt to the enforcement respondent;
(3) If enforcement is conducted in a different location, or involves search and seizure, small claims, or emergency situations requiring direct receipt of cash or instruments by enforcement personnel, a receipt shall be promptly issued to the payer, and the items shall be promptly handed over to the court's finance department;
(4) It is strictly prohibited to illegally charge fees to the enforcement applicant or the enforcement respondent.

Article 66. Payment of Enforcement Funds
(1) Handle the settlement procedures for enforcement costs and enforcement funds within the specified time limit, and promptly notify the enforcement applicant to complete the withdrawal procedures;
(2) If payment needs to be delayed, provide a written explanation of the reasons before the deadline expires and submit for approval by the relevant leadership;
(3) If the enforcement applicant entrusts or designates another person to receive payment, verify whether the authorization documents are complete and valid, and require the recipient to present lawful and valid receipts.

Article 67. Enforcement Respondent Refusing to Perform on the Grounds of Alleged Errors in the Substantive or Procedural Aspects of the Effective Legal Document
(1) If the effective legal document is indeed erroneous, inform the party that they may apply for retrial under the trial supervision procedure or apply to the relevant court for correction, and promptly report to the leadership;
(2) If the effective legal document is not erroneous, promptly provide explanations and continue enforcement.

Article 68. Relevant Departments and Individuals Failing to Assist in Enforcement
(1) Inform them of the relevant legal provisions and provide persuasion and education;
(2) If they still refuse to assist, take relevant compulsory measures in accordance with the law.

VII. Handling of Litigation-Related Petitions

Article 69. Basic Requirements
(1) Attach great importance to and handle litigation-related petitions conscientiously, effectively protecting the lawful rights and interests of petitioners;
(2) Handle petition matters promptly, striving to ensure that visitors are received, letters are responded to, and appeals are replied to;
(3) Receive petitioners in a civilized manner in accordance with the law, maintaining the good image of the People's Courts.

Article 70. Handling of Incoming Letters
(1) Review them promptly and register them in accordance with the regulations; do not suppress or delay handling privately;
(2) If a reply or return of relevant materials is necessary, do so promptly;
(3) If forwarding to relevant departments or lower courts is necessary, do so promptly.

Article 71. Reception of Visitors
(1) Receive visitors promptly, listen patiently to their opinions, and keep records;
(2) If the issue can be resolved on the spot, provide an immediate answer; if not, accept the materials and inform them to wait for the outcome within the agreed time limit.

Article 72. Visitors Who Are Elderly, Weak, Ill, Disabled, or Pregnant
(1) Give priority to reception;
(2) If the visitor requests assistance, help contact a social relief station as appropriate;
(3) If an unexpected situation occurs while receiving a visitor, take appropriate rescue measures immediately.

Article 73. Group Visits
(1) Report to the leadership, arrange reception promptly, and contact relevant departments for joint handling;
(2) Depending on the situation, instruct the group to select one to five representatives to explain the purpose and reason of the visit;
(3) Calm the visitors' emotions and provide guidance.

Article 74. Petition Matters Not Within the Court's Jurisdiction
Inform the petitioner that the court has no authority to handle the matter and explain the reasons; based on the content of the petition, indicate the authority that has jurisdiction.

Article 75. Petition Matters Involving State Secrets, Commercial Secrets, or Personal Privacy
(1) Properly safeguard materials involving secrets and personal privacy;
(2) Conscientiously comply with relevant provisions; do not disclose or use state secrets, commercial secrets, or personal privacy obtained during petition work.

Article 76. Petitioner Reporting Issues Such as Unfair Judgments, Ineffective Enforcement, or Trial Style in the Local Court
(1) Carefully record the circumstances reported by the petitioner;
(2) If the petitioner is dissatisfied with the court's judgment, inform them that they may appeal, petition, or apply for retrial in accordance with the law;
(3) If other issues are reported, promptly forward the materials to the relevant court department for handling.

Article 77. Petitioner Repeatedly Writing or Visiting to Urge the Outcome
(1) Inform the prescribed processing time limit and advise them to wait patiently for the outcome;
(2) If the situation is urgent, promptly inform the person in charge or the responsible department;
(3) If the time limit has been exceeded, inform them of the reasons.

Article 78. Petitioner Dissatisfied with the Outcome and Requesting Re-Handling
(1) If the handling is indeed improper, promptly report to the leadership and correct it in accordance with regulations;
(2) If the handling outcome is correct, provide relevant explanations and detail the procedures and basis.

Article 79. Visitor Stating They Will Remain in the Court or Take Other Extreme Measures If the Issue Is Not Resolved
(1) Promptly dissuade and educate, avoiding inappropriate words or actions that may provoke the visitor;
(2) Immediately report to the leadership and actively take appropriate measures to prevent accidents.

VIII. Extracurricular Activities

Article 80. Basic Requirements
(1) Observe social ethics and abide by the law;
(2) Strengthen self-cultivation and exercise strict self-discipline;
(3) Restrain extracurricular speech and conduct, eliminate bad habits and behaviors inconsistent with the image of a judge that may affect the impartial performance of duties, and consciously maintain the image of a judge.

Article 81. Invitations to Seminars or Research Activities
(1) Refuse invitations from government agencies, enterprises, law firms, intermediary institutions, etc., that have an interest in a case;
(2) Invitations from Party, government, military organs, academic groups, or mass organizations that have no interest in a case may be accepted only after obtaining approval from the unit.

Article 82. Invitations to Join Various Social Organizations or Fellowship Activities
(1) If it is truly necessary to join a social organization registered with the civil affairs department at any level, promptly report and obtain approval from the court where the judge works according to the judge management authority;
(2) Do not join for-profit social organizations;
(3) Do not accept invitations for meals, gifts, or cash that are inconsistent with the requirement of integrity and self-discipline.

Article 83. Engaging in Writing, Teaching, and Other Activities
(1) Provided it does not affect trial work, spare time may be used for writing, teaching, and other activities;
(2) During writing and teaching, avoid commenting on specific cases or related parties; do not disclose or use state secrets, commercial secrets, personal privacy, or other non-public information obtained during work;
(3) Lawful remuneration obtained from activities outside judicial duties shall be taxed in accordance with the law.

Article 84. Accepting Interviews with News Media Related to Court Work
(1) Interviews with news media must be arranged or approved by the organization;
(2) During interviews, do not make statements that harm judicial impartiality; do not comment on cases under trial or related parties; do not disclose state secrets, commercial secrets, personal privacy, or other non-public information obtained during work.

Article 85. Disputes with Others Involving the Judge or Their Relatives and Friends
(1) Remain calm and restrained, and resolve the dispute through lawful and proper channels;
(2) Do not use the status of a judge to seek special treatment; do not obstruct the resolution of the issue by relevant departments.

Article 86. Disputes Involving the Judge or Their Family Members Requiring Resolution through Litigation
(1) For cases involving the judge themselves or cases in which they participate as an agent for a direct relative, participate in the litigation equally in accordance with the relevant legal provisions;
(2) Do not seek special treatment based on the status of a judge during the litigation process; do not use one's position to collect necessary evidence;
(3) For litigation cases of other family members who are not direct relatives, generally allow them to retain their own litigation agent; the judge should not serve as a litigation agent in such proceedings.

Article 87. Precautions in Social Settings
(1) When participating in social activities, consciously maintain the image of a judge;
(2) It is strictly prohibited to enter commercial entertainment venues in police vehicles or wearing uniforms.

Article 88. Family or Friends Inviting Participation in Feudal Superstitious Activities
(1) Do not participate in cult organizations or engage in feudal superstitious activities;
(2) Promote science to family and friends, guiding them to believe in science and oppose feudal superstition;
(3) If any person uses feudal superstitious activities to commit crimes, immediately report to the relevant organizations and public security departments.

Article 89. Traveling Abroad for Personal Reasons (e.g., Visiting Relatives, Tourism)
(1) Honestly report to the organization the countries or regions to be visited and the date of return; travel only after obtaining the organization's consent;
(2) Return to work on time;
(3) Comply with local laws and respect local customs, traditions, and religious practices;
(4) Pay attention to personal image and uphold national dignity.

IX. Supervision and Sanctions

Article 90. People's Courts at all levels shall strictly require and supervise their judges to comply with these Norms; specifically, the political departments and discipline inspection and supervision departments of each court shall be responsible.

Article 91. Higher People's Courts shall guide and supervise lower People's Courts in the implementation of these Norms; the Supreme People's Court shall guide and supervise local People's Courts at all levels in the implementation of these Norms.

Article 92. Local People's Courts at all levels shall, based on their actual circumstances, study and formulate specific implementing rules or measures, and effectively strengthen the training and assessment of these Norms.

Article 93. The vast majority of judges in People's Courts at all levels shall conscientiously abide by and implement these Norms. Those who violate these Norms, if the circumstances are minor and there are no harmful consequences, shall be given admonishing talks and criticism education; if the conduct constitutes a disciplinary violation, it shall be dealt with in accordance with the relevant disciplinary provisions of the People's Court; if the conduct constitutes a violation of law, it shall be dealt with seriously in accordance with legal provisions.

X. Supplementary Provisions

Article 94. People's Assessors and other staff of the People's Courts shall refer to these Norms for implementation; after retirement, judges shall refer to the relevant requirements of these Norms to constrain their speech and conduct.

Article 95. These Norms shall be interpreted by the Supreme People's Court.

Article 96. These Norms shall come into effect on the date of issuance. The "Code of Conduct for Judges (Trial)" issued by the Supreme People's Court on November 4, 2005, shall be simultaneously repealed.
